WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:04.899
- Reporting live from the end of CES.

00:00:04.899 --> 00:00:07.429
This happened two days ago, this episode.

00:00:07.429 --> 00:00:08.816
Babin and I were just walking,

00:00:08.816 --> 00:00:10.090
and he said to me, "Fuck, dude.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:11.717
"I don't think we can put any of it out."

00:00:11.717 --> 00:00:13.936
There was so much important information,

00:00:13.936 --> 00:00:15.346
like secret information, it was just

00:00:15.346 --> 00:00:17.933
something we couldn't put out.

00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:19.056
That's today's episode.

00:00:19.056 --> 00:00:22.073
Bottom line is like, some days

00:00:22.073 --> 00:00:24.133
it just fucking works
out that way. (laughs)

00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:24.966
Enjoy it.

00:00:24.966 --> 00:00:28.466
(dramatic rhythmic music)

00:00:31.609 --> 00:00:35.692
("The Coast" by Sample and Hold)

00:00:41.913 --> 00:00:45.637
Hey, guys, CES 2018 off to a good start.

00:00:45.637 --> 00:00:47.844
Some good meetings, called Andrea,

00:00:47.844 --> 00:00:49.285
our new Chief Client Officer.

00:00:49.285 --> 00:00:50.118
She's right there.

00:00:50.118 --> 00:00:51.526
(Andrea gasps)
- [Voiceover] Back seat!

00:00:51.526 --> 00:00:52.658
- (laughs) And so it's going well.

00:00:52.658 --> 00:00:55.601
We're going to a dinner party right now

00:00:55.601 --> 00:00:58.184
that we put on and little wine,

00:00:59.852 --> 00:01:02.818
little senior executives,
a little networking,

00:01:02.818 --> 00:01:04.397
and then the MediaLink Party,

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:05.426
which I'm not sure I'm gonna go to.

00:01:05.426 --> 00:01:06.776
Last night was a pretty exciting night.

00:01:06.776 --> 00:01:08.987
Do not understand, if I lose tonight?

00:01:08.987 --> 00:01:10.404
It's even better.

00:01:11.324 --> 00:01:12.568
And so I didn't sleep actually.

00:01:12.568 --> 00:01:13.990
I was just excitement,
which never happens,

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:15.819
so I'm gonna try to get some rest

00:01:15.819 --> 00:01:17.254
'cause it's a long week, but I'll sneak

00:01:17.254 --> 00:01:20.157
in there real quick, kiss some babies.

00:01:20.157 --> 00:01:22.141
It's (laughs) I'm gonna
get mobbed this week.

00:01:22.141 --> 00:01:22.974
(laughter)

00:01:22.974 --> 00:01:23.807
- So need to be careful with myself.

00:01:23.807 --> 00:01:24.950
- Here we are.
- See ya.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:26.016
How are the kids?

00:01:26.016 --> 00:01:26.849
- [Shelley] Everyone is really good.

00:01:26.849 --> 00:01:27.682
- Everyone's good?
- Everyone is really--

00:01:27.682 --> 00:01:29.147
Jay?
- Good holiday?

00:01:29.147 --> 00:01:31.760
- You have changed his life, truly.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:32.971
- So awesome.
He,

00:01:32.971 --> 00:01:34.040
not knowing that we

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:34.998
were connected--
- I know, I know.

00:01:34.998 --> 00:01:36.339
- He's been following
you, you realize that?

00:01:36.339 --> 00:01:37.239
- Yes, yes, yes.
- He said to me,

00:01:37.239 --> 00:01:38.968
"Mom," do you know Gary Vee?

00:01:38.968 --> 00:01:40.027
- Yes, I remember this story.

00:01:40.027 --> 00:01:40.971
- Okay?

00:01:40.971 --> 00:01:45.273
And now he writes like Gary
Vee on his LinkedIn, and as--

00:01:45.273 --> 00:01:46.478
- I saw that, but there are

00:01:46.478 --> 00:01:47.630
words!
- 15,000

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:49.042
views in a minute?
- (laughs) I love it!

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:50.521
- Like something like that, like--

00:01:50.521 --> 00:01:51.494
- I love it.
- He's not

00:01:51.494 --> 00:01:52.910
the Gary Vee status yet,
but I gotta tell you,

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:54.154
watch him.
- On his way?

00:01:54.154 --> 00:01:55.491
- 'Cause he's damn close.
- I better--

00:01:55.491 --> 00:01:56.842
Time to get back to work!
- You better watch out!

00:01:56.842 --> 00:01:58.323
- Let's go, Babs!

00:01:58.323 --> 00:02:00.021
I love it, good for him.
- He's catching up,

00:02:00.021 --> 00:02:02.603
and his confidence, I mean he always had

00:02:02.603 --> 00:02:05.364
that confidence--
- Yeah, but now it's--

00:02:05.364 --> 00:02:09.208
- It's an educated confidence, you know?

00:02:09.208 --> 00:02:10.104
- Yeah!

00:02:10.104 --> 00:02:12.545
- And so it's really quite remarkable.

00:02:12.545 --> 00:02:13.503
- That makes me happy.
- You know, you gotta

00:02:13.503 --> 00:02:16.565
bottle that, so I really
cannot thank you enough.

00:02:16.565 --> 00:02:18.540
- That makes me super happy, but listen,

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.213
we can create the
framework, but it takes them

00:02:21.213 --> 00:02:22.274
to take advantage of it.
- Absolutely.

00:02:22.274 --> 00:02:24.409
- You know, he did.
- But even--

00:02:24.409 --> 00:02:26.018
- Here we are in a partnership dinner

00:02:26.018 --> 00:02:27.784
with the Female Quotient, Shelley Zalis

00:02:27.784 --> 00:02:30.395
is one of the most interesting and dynamic

00:02:30.395 --> 00:02:32.395
executives that I know in the game,

00:02:32.395 --> 00:02:34.589
and VaynerX, PureWow, and VaynerMedia

00:02:34.589 --> 00:02:37.969
have teamed up with FQ,
the Female Quotient,

00:02:37.969 --> 00:02:40.787
or the Girls' Lounge,
all her incredible energy

00:02:40.787 --> 00:02:43.537
on the most important issues like

00:02:44.909 --> 00:02:47.903
quality, not only on the
female scale, but just life.

00:02:47.903 --> 00:02:49.587
Just an incredibly fascinating woman

00:02:49.587 --> 00:02:52.084
that I've gotten to know
over the last five years,

00:02:52.084 --> 00:02:54.329
who I have an enormous
amount of respect for.

00:02:54.329 --> 00:02:58.637
Sharp as a tack, heart
of gold, big time brain.

00:02:58.637 --> 00:03:00.518
Just love her energy, character.

00:03:00.518 --> 00:03:03.658
Wait 'til you see her
puffy (laughs) pink coat,

00:03:03.658 --> 00:03:05.083
but nonetheless, we're gonna do a little

00:03:05.083 --> 00:03:06.936
fireside chat here and talk about

00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:08.680
the quality issues in the marketing world

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:10.184
as we see across the world, and how do you

00:03:10.184 --> 00:03:12.434
not over-pander, how do
you not under-pander?

00:03:12.434 --> 00:03:15.190
Should be a good, great question

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:17.823
to a great group, and I like these

00:03:17.823 --> 00:03:19.534
kinda small dinners
where you get to think,

00:03:19.534 --> 00:03:21.228
and that's what tonight'll be about.

00:03:21.228 --> 00:03:23.286
I think, you know--

00:03:23.286 --> 00:03:24.456
The thing that's been running through

00:03:24.456 --> 00:03:26.176
my mind quite a bit around change

00:03:26.176 --> 00:03:28.843
is respectful disrespect, right?

00:03:31.337 --> 00:03:33.121
You know, you were talking about--

00:03:33.121 --> 00:03:34.203
This is a true scene, we actually

00:03:34.203 --> 00:03:35.509
have it on film in the back room

00:03:35.509 --> 00:03:36.572
before this started.
- Oh, no!

00:03:36.572 --> 00:03:39.430
- We're talking about
the main stage at CES

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.517
and the issues that I'm
sure we'll get to tonight.

00:03:42.517 --> 00:03:43.665
And I literally asked Shelley,

00:03:43.665 --> 00:03:46.575
I'm like, "Where is the main stage?"

00:03:46.575 --> 00:03:47.432
(Shelley and audience laugh)

00:03:47.432 --> 00:03:48.315
What is the main stage?

00:03:48.315 --> 00:03:50.371
I've been coming to CES for six years.

00:03:50.371 --> 00:03:52.917
I've never gone to the conference.

00:03:52.917 --> 00:03:53.900
(laughter)

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.298
I didn't know there was a main stage

00:03:56.298 --> 00:03:58.941
at CES until some of my friends texted me

00:03:58.941 --> 00:04:02.774
about like, let's break
the main stage, right?

00:04:03.636 --> 00:04:05.247
In that moment back there, I'm like,

00:04:05.247 --> 00:04:06.897
"It's respectful disrespect."

00:04:06.897 --> 00:04:08.953
I'm not disrespecting CES.

00:04:08.953 --> 00:04:11.544
I'm not mad at the status quo.

00:04:11.544 --> 00:04:13.893
I'm just not spending any time on it

00:04:13.893 --> 00:04:15.459
because the only way you can change

00:04:15.459 --> 00:04:18.800
is when you don't accept
what's already been accepted.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:20.720
You're just only focused on one thing,

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.762
which is the reality of the marketplace,

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:25.099
the young consumer, whatever
you're doing for a living.

00:04:25.099 --> 00:04:28.061
So for me I think, you know, listen,

00:04:28.061 --> 00:04:29.658
when you're an entrepreneur, you don't

00:04:29.658 --> 00:04:31.475
have somebody judging you,
the results judge you,

00:04:31.475 --> 00:04:33.196
and so maybe it's been easier for me

00:04:33.196 --> 00:04:34.900
than a lot of you that are executives,

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:36.693
so I have a lot of empathy to that.

00:04:36.693 --> 00:04:40.111
But I do think that respectful disrespect

00:04:40.111 --> 00:04:43.425
is what comes to mind in
making change, which is

00:04:43.425 --> 00:04:45.916
I'm fascinated by people looking backwards

00:04:45.916 --> 00:04:48.486
because nothing that happened yesterday

00:04:48.486 --> 00:04:50.285
is what gets you to the next place

00:04:50.285 --> 00:04:52.083
except the thematics, but the details

00:04:52.083 --> 00:04:54.336
and the execution change, and so

00:04:54.336 --> 00:04:56.709
to me, when we were talking about this,

00:04:56.709 --> 00:04:59.383
I asked you, "Who picks
the people for the panel?"

00:04:59.383 --> 00:05:00.841
(laughter)

00:05:00.841 --> 00:05:02.530
This stuff is so simple!
- (laughing) He did say that!

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:05.430
- I have a lot of CEOs
who are always like,

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.643
"I'm mad at my
salespeople," and I ask her,

00:05:07.643 --> 00:05:10.276
"Well, change their bonus structure."

00:05:10.276 --> 00:05:12.417
You can get any salesperson
to do anything you want

00:05:12.417 --> 00:05:15.146
in the world by making the bonuses

00:05:15.146 --> 00:05:16.864
reflect what you want to happen.

00:05:16.864 --> 00:05:19.629
If you wanna do more
Facebook stuff, make an

00:05:19.629 --> 00:05:21.381
arbitrary bonus that if you do seven

00:05:21.381 --> 00:05:24.780
Facebook things, you
receive 80% of your bonus.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:26.326
And then everybody will do that,

00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:29.121
and so for me, I think we shouldn't

00:05:29.121 --> 00:05:30.831
even talk about this tonight.

00:05:30.831 --> 00:05:32.580
I think we need to figure out who's

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:36.334
picking it, and mandate, and move on.

00:05:36.334 --> 00:05:38.875
- But there's been a lot of conversation.

00:05:38.875 --> 00:05:40.428
I don't want to say controversy,

00:05:40.428 --> 00:05:43.049
but a lot of conversation with CES

00:05:43.049 --> 00:05:45.826
this year on women on the main stage.

00:05:45.826 --> 00:05:48.497
I think you've all seen a lot of press

00:05:48.497 --> 00:05:50.521
and more importantly, a lot of tweets

00:05:50.521 --> 00:05:53.772
around it, and people kinda arguing

00:05:53.772 --> 00:05:57.182
about why this and why that, and so

00:05:57.182 --> 00:06:00.735
Steven has been instrumental with UM

00:06:00.735 --> 00:06:03.860
and CBS Interactive, so I wanna thank

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:05.197
all of our amazing
(Gary applauds)

00:06:05.197 --> 00:06:06.653
partners for bold leadership.

00:06:06.653 --> 00:06:09.204
(applause)

00:06:09.204 --> 00:06:11.037
We'll be having--

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:13.286
- I agree 100%, I love the concept.

00:06:13.286 --> 00:06:14.844
What we're gonna do is go around.

00:06:14.844 --> 00:06:15.840
We're gonna give you a little bit of time

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:17.848
to think about this, but this is not about

00:06:17.848 --> 00:06:19.366
four to seven, or eleven people

00:06:19.366 --> 00:06:21.543
getting up here and taking the mic.

00:06:21.543 --> 00:06:22.682
When you look at the pedigree of everybody

00:06:22.682 --> 00:06:23.615
in this room, whether they're in

00:06:23.615 --> 00:06:25.338
the beginning of their
career or later in it,

00:06:25.338 --> 00:06:26.683
everybody here has something to add,

00:06:26.683 --> 00:06:29.388
so we're gonna go around
and ask everybody for what

00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:31.600
their point of view is,
as an actionable item

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:33.975
to make this better,
and take all 50 thoughts

00:06:33.975 --> 00:06:38.103
and consolidate them and create an output.

00:06:38.103 --> 00:06:39.860
That's what we wanna challenge
everybody to think about.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:41.173
Like what's the actionable item,

00:06:41.173 --> 00:06:44.994
like if you're the Lord
of executing this fix.

00:06:44.994 --> 00:06:45.975
- Or the Lordess.

00:06:45.975 --> 00:06:47.809
- Or the Lordess of fixing--
- Just saying.

00:06:47.809 --> 00:06:51.441
- Just saying, of executing this fix,

00:06:51.441 --> 00:06:52.914
what is your point of view?

00:06:52.914 --> 00:06:57.081
(soft, inspirational electronic music)

00:07:11.853 --> 00:07:14.637
And I've just been listening, and I think

00:07:14.637 --> 00:07:16.108
about things quite a bit, about

00:07:16.108 --> 00:07:19.584
in a very basic term
of offense and defense.

00:07:19.584 --> 00:07:20.684
I think a lot of what we're talking

00:07:20.684 --> 00:07:23.476
about tonight is defense, or reacting.

00:07:23.476 --> 00:07:26.363
So Battle of the Sexes, Billie Jean King

00:07:26.363 --> 00:07:29.577
did not convince the
UTA to change the rules.

00:07:29.577 --> 00:07:32.782
They created the Virginia
Slims Tournament.

00:07:32.782 --> 00:07:34.333
Nice, but we're not
talking about the reality

00:07:34.333 --> 00:07:35.889
of that situation, which is they built

00:07:35.889 --> 00:07:38.830
a different thing, which
they were in control of,

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:41.506
and so whether it's my idea of dictating

00:07:41.506 --> 00:07:44.900
or grassroots, it's still,
there's a gatekeeper.

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:46.161
We need to understand real life.

00:07:46.161 --> 00:07:48.470
I think everybody here
does, and so instead

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:51.456
of pandering to the
gatekeeper, to me it's binary.

00:07:51.456 --> 00:07:53.447
Either we go back to let's figure out

00:07:53.447 --> 00:07:55.686
who the 54 gatekeepers
are and collectively

00:07:55.686 --> 00:07:58.718
put pressure on them, and/or create

00:07:58.718 --> 00:08:01.897
our own thing and all go there.

00:08:01.897 --> 00:08:05.035
I really just genuinely
believe it's A or B.

00:08:05.035 --> 00:08:06.861
One of my favorite
things about even coming

00:08:06.861 --> 00:08:08.528
to events like this and then micro-events

00:08:08.528 --> 00:08:11.576
within events like this,
is meeting new people.

00:08:11.576 --> 00:08:13.616
So fun little thing on your way out

00:08:13.616 --> 00:08:15.872
to the elevator, I think
there's a lot of people here

00:08:15.872 --> 00:08:18.137
that don't know each other that should,

00:08:18.137 --> 00:08:20.126
so one face that you don't recognize,

00:08:20.126 --> 00:08:21.331
and say, "Hello," I think would be

00:08:21.331 --> 00:08:23.911
a really good, tangible execution.

00:08:23.911 --> 00:08:26.647
Hope you have a great
CES and a healthy 2018.

00:08:26.647 --> 00:08:27.480
Thanks for coming.

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:30.801
(applause)
(indistinct chatter)

00:08:30.801 --> 00:08:32.130
(dramatic piano music)

